			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_3 et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_10">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_21 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_4 mini-head et_pb_text_align_center et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>What Causes AC Problems? A Closer Look</h2></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_11">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_22 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_5 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Low Refrigerant Levels</h3>
<p>A refrigerant leak drops system pressure, which can cause the evaporator coil to get too cold. Moisture in the air then freezes on the coil, leading to ice buildup — and reduced cooling in the meantime. This needs a professional to locate and fix the actual leak before recharging the system; simply adding refrigerant without finding the leak just delays the same failure, and it will happen again.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_23 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_6 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Dirty Air Filters</h3>
<p>Clogged filters restrict airflow, which forces the evaporator coil to run too cold and can cause it to freeze over — and separately makes the whole system work harder, driving up energy bills in the process. Replace filters every 1–3 months, and more often if you have pets or allergy sufferers in the home. This is the single easiest thing a homeowner can do to prevent an unnecessary service call.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_12">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_24 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_7 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Electrical or Component Issues</h3>
<p>Failed capacitors, contactors, or relays can cause a system that powers on but doesn’t actually cool the home. This is one of the more common reasons for a no-cooling call, and it requires a trained technician to diagnose and repair safely — electrical components in an AC system carry real risk if handled without the right training.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div><div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_25 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_8 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Thermostat Malfunctions</h3>
<p>A thermostat that isn’t reading temperature accurately can keep the system running longer than it should — which, combined with any of the issues above, accelerates coil freezing and adds unnecessary wear on the compressor. Sometimes a reset or recalibration resolves it; sometimes the thermostat itself needs replacing.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_13">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_26 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_9 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h3 style="text-align: center;">Poor Airflow</h3>
<p>Beyond dirty filters, blocked vents or improperly sealed ductwork can starve the system of airflow it needs to operate correctly. If you’ve already ruled out filters and airflow is still weak, it’s time for a professional <a href="/air-duct-repair-replacement/">air duct evaluation</a>.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div>
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_4 et_pb_with_background et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_14">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_4_4 et_pb_column_27 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough et-last-child">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_10 mini-head et_pb_text_align_center et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>How Much Does AC Repair Cost?</h2>
<p style="text-align: left;">Repair costs depend on the specific issue, the age of the unit, and the parts required. As a general guideline, AC repairs can range from minor fixes, such as a capacitor replacement, to more extensive work, such as a compressor replacement or major electrical repair.<br />We always provide a clear, upfront estimate before any work begins — you’ll never be surprised by the final invoice. If a repair doesn’t make financial sense (for example, on a unit near end of life), we’ll tell you honestly rather than recommend a repair that won’t hold. Ask about our <a href="/financing/">financing options</a> if a larger repair or replacement is needed.</p></div>
			</div>
			</div>
				
				
				
				
			</div>
				
				
			</div><div class="et_pb_section et_pb_section_5 et_section_regular">
				
				
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_row et_pb_row_15">
				<div class="et_pb_column et_pb_column_1_2 et_pb_column_28 et_pb_css_mix_blend_mode_passthrough">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_with_border et_pb_module et_pb_text et_pb_text_11 mini-head et_pb_text_align_left et_pb_bg_layout_light">
				
				
				
				
				<div class="et_pb_text_inner"><h2>Why Timely AC Repairs Matter</h2>
<p>Small issues left unaddressed compound into bigger, more expensive ones. Getting repairs done promptly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Improves energy efficiency, which lowers your monthly utility bills</li>
<li>Extends the overall lifespan of your system</li>
<li>Enhances day-to-day comfort throughout the home</li>
<li>Prevents larger, costlier breakdowns down the road</li>
<li>Improves indoor air quality by keeping the system running as designed</li>
<li>Reduces the odds of an emergency, no-AC-in-August breakdown</li>
<li>Keeps your system operating safely</li>
</ul></div>
			</div>
